// Merge-coverage guard: hold release-note publication whenever the host's scan
// of merged pull requests is anything other than a completed scan. The host
// publishes its five-value coverage vocabulary as five independent 0/1 facts -
// MERGE_COVERAGE_AVAILABLE, _EMPTY, _UNAVAILABLE, _ERROR, _STALE - exactly one
// of which is 1 on any bar. `empty` means the forge answered and there was
// nothing to count; `unavailable` and `error` mean it did not answer.
// The rule names the two states that PERMIT publication and holds on
// everything else. That is an allow-list, and it is the whole point: a sixth
// coverage state added to the host tomorrow publishes 0 for both of these and
// is held, where a rule naming _UNAVAILABLE / _ERROR / _STALE would wave the
// new state straight through. Same posture as WATCHDOG_OPS in
// nano/watchdog/profile.py, adopted here for the same reason.
// This rule gates release-note publication and nothing else. It must never be
// wired to a branch, a deploy, or an engineering merge: "the merge list could
// not be read" is not a reason to stop shipping software, only a reason to
// stop claiming the notes cover everything that shipped.
// REGIME: all of them. Coverage is a property of the host's own scan, not of
// whatever the scan is looking at.
// CONDITIONS: the host publishes exactly one coverage fact as 1 on every bar,
// and derives it from the API response rather than from the parsed result. An
// error path that returns an empty list must publish MERGE_COVERAGE_ERROR and
// never MERGE_COVERAGE_EMPTY - that substitution is the outage this rule
// exists to catch.
// INVALIDATION: the scan completing. The rule does not latch and does not
// decide when the hold ends; the host does.
// SHAPE: 5m, the cadence the merge scan itself runs on. Reading coverage
// faster than the scan that produces it only re-reads the same answer.
// NOT aether_release_notes_batch_unaccounted: that rule reads the count and
// fires when the scan worked and found merges nobody wrote up. This one fires
// when there is no trustworthy count to read, and it deliberately does not
// read the count - an outage must never arrive at the batch rule as a zero.
// CALIBRATED ON: nothing numeric; both comparisons are against the host's own
// booleans. What does not travel is the vocabulary itself. A host with a
// different set of coverage states publishes its own and re-derives which of
// them are complete scans before reusing this rule.
strategy AetherReleaseNotesCoverage {

    agent ReleaseDesk

    every 5m {

        if MERGE_COVERAGE_AVAILABLE <= 0 and MERGE_COVERAGE_EMPTY <= 0 {

            pause()

        }

    }

}
